    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a per-city capacity rule that rejects `POST /api/owners` with **409** when the target city already contains 50 or more owners. Following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions:\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectFullOwnerCity.java` \u2014 a `Reject`-style pipeline step reading the request DTO (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`), counting the city's owners, and throwing a checked `CityAtCapacityException` at the cap (`CITY_CAPACITY = 50`).\n- `OwnerCities.java` \u2014 a small shared helper defining \"owners in a city\" (case-insensitive), mirroring the existing `OwnerHouseholds` pattern.\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` + `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 checked exception and its 409 `ProblemDetail` responder, matching the existing duplicate-telephone/household handlers.\n- `...CityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 escalation wiring for the handler.\n\n**Changed files:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `rejectFullCity` step after `validate` (so a missing/blank city is still a 400 first) and before the rest of the pipeline, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java` \u2014 replaced its private `citySize` with the new shared `OwnerCities.size(...)`, so the capacity check and the customer-code sequence count a city identically instead of duplicating that logic.\n\nEach new class has a single responsibility, and the counting logic now lives in one shared place. `AcceptanceTest` (the city-capacity checkpoint) and the full test suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
